<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The styles the screens actually ask for — step 22 / 7b, §5c item 10.

    ⚠⚠ **THE TWO STYLES BELOW WERE REFERENCED TWENTY TIMES AND DEFINED NOWHERE.** Every basket row
    label carries `Style="{DynamicResource ListItemDetailTextStyle}"`, and there was no such key in
    the application — `DynamicResource` to a missing key **silently applies nothing**, so the money
    screen's rows have never been styled at all. Found 2026-08-18 by listing every resource the XAML
    references and checking each one exists; the two that did not are these.

    ⚠⚠ **AND THIS IS WHERE MAUI STARTS CONSUMING THE PORTAL'S COLOURS.** The plumbing was finished
    long ago — the portal assigns a scheme, the backend resolves it, `Theming.Apply` writes the seven
    `Theme*` keys into `Application.Resources` — and **nothing in any XAML file read one of them**
    (Matt, 2026-08-18: *"the colours do NOT appear to have changed when I set a new colour scheme"*;
    the immediate cause was an empty `TillThemeAssignments`, but had it been populated, only Store
    Information would have moved). Defining the missing styles from the slots fixes both faults with
    one change, and its blast radius is exactly the elements that already asked for these keys.

    ⚠⚠ **`DynamicResource`, NEVER `StaticResource`, for a theme slot.** `StaticResource` resolves once
    at parse time, so a control would keep the palette that was current when its page was built and a
    scheme applied afterwards would move some controls and not others. **Half a themed screen is worse
    than none** — the same hazard `Theming.cs`'s own header records.

    ⚠⚠ **INK AND SURFACE ARE APPLIED AS A PAIR, ALWAYS.** Setting a text colour without owning the
    colour behind it is how Store Information shipped with every label light-grey on near-white and
    unreadable (build 1.74.0) — and it is how a dark-mode device would render `ThemeInk` (#272643,
    near-black) on its own near-black background. `Theming.Apply` sets `UserAppTheme` from the theme's
    base mode, so a scheme decides light or dark; a screen that takes its ink from a slot **must** take
    its surface from one too. See `till-design.md` D5's sibling rule.
-->
<?xaml-comp compile="true" ?>
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">

    <!-- ⚠ The primary row text — quantity, and anything that is the row's subject. -->
    <Style x:Key="ListItemTextStyle" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalTextAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠ The row's detail text — name, price, tax.

        ⚠⚠ IT USES `ThemeInk`, **NOT** `ThemeInkMuted`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. "Detail" here means
        *the rest of the row*, not *less important*: the price and the VAT column are on it. Muting the
        price of an item is precisely the fault that made Store Information unreadable, and on the
        basket it would mute the numbers the customer is being charged. ⚠ `ThemeInkMuted` is for
        genuine asides — hints, empty-state notes, "(waiting to send)".
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListItemDetailTextStyle" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalTextAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠ For genuine asides, and the one style that IS muted. Not yet used by any screen — it exists
        so that the next person who wants grey text takes it from the theme instead of typing a hex,
        which is how the unreadable-label fault got in.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="MutedTextStyle" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠ A page that owns its own background, so the ink above is guaranteed something to sit on —
        see the pairing rule at the top of this file. Applied per page rather than as an implicit
        `ContentPage` style: an implicit style would repaint every screen in the app at once, including
        ones nobody has looked at, on a UI framework where nobody can currently run the app.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ThemedPageStyle" TargetType="ContentPage">
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
